// White Yarrow 'Achillea' — Achillea millefolium
// Edit this file to update plant-specific content.

#import "../../template.typ": *

#plant-header("White Yarrow 'Achillea'", "Achillea millefolium", "yarrow")[
  White Yarrow is a tough, drought-adapted native perennial with flat-topped flower clusters that attract pollinators abundantly. Its feathery, aromatic foliage grows 18–24 inches tall. Requires no feeding and thrives in lean, dry conditions. Established 2+ years — on drip irrigation. NOTE: Drainage is critical — clay soil is a significant challenge for this plant.
]

=== Watering

On drip irrigation with a *0.5 gph emitter*. See the Irrigation & Watering section for the shared drip schedule. White Yarrow is very drought tolerant and roots are susceptible to rot in persistently wet clay soil. The combination of small emitter output and clay's moisture retention keeps the root zone appropriately moist without overwatering. Stop drip support in September.

=== Fertilization

Yarrow thrives in poor to average soil and performs best with no fertilization — this aligns perfectly with your high-P soil test. Do not fertilize. Rich soil and excess nitrogen cause leggy, floppy plants. If your clay soil is very lean in organic matter, a very thin compost topdressing in spring is the maximum it should receive.

=== Pruning & Maintenance

Deadhead spent flowers to prolong blooming and prevent excessive self-seeding. Cut back by one-third after the first flush to encourage a compact second flush. Cut to the ground in late fall or leave standing for winter interest. Divide clumps every 2–3 years in spring to prevent spreading and rejuvenate flowering.

=== Wildlife Value

Yarrow is an outstanding pollinator plant. The flat-topped white flower heads are perfect landing platforms for butterflies (especially skippers, cabbage whites, and sulfurs), native bees, and beneficial parasitic wasps throughout summer. These beneficial insects in turn help control garden pests. Seed-eating birds including house finches and American robins may visit spent seed heads in late summer and fall. Cottontail rabbits typically avoid yarrow due to its bitter, aromatic foliage — this makes it one of the more rabbit-resistant perennials in the landscape.

=== Pests & Disease

Yarrow is rarely troubled by pests in Colorado. #link("https://www.google.com/search?q=yarrow+powdery+mildew+treatment")[Powdery mildew] can occur in humid conditions — improve air circulation through division and thinning. #link("https://www.google.com/search?q=spittlebug+treatment")[Spittlebugs] create frothy masses on stems; they are cosmetic only — a strong water spray dislodges them. In general, yarrow's aromatic oils deter most insects. Drainage-related crown rot (see Other Care) is the primary threat, not pest insects.

=== Other Care

CLAY DRAINAGE NOTE: Plant on a slight slope or raised mound and amend with coarse grit to ensure water moves away from the crown. Yarrow will perform significantly better with improved drainage even though it is otherwise extremely tough. Clumps spread by rhizome and self-seed — plan for a naturalized colony effect over time.
